\section{Abstract}
This work provides a preliminary design for a mission towards Umbriel conceived to
test the efficacy of Optimization Methods. To support the minimization of the fuel consumption we use gravity assists on two planets to reach our final destination. 
A thorough analysis of the mathematical models was carried out to optimize the effectiveness of the low thrust trajectory phase. The results provide a maneuver lasting 34 days, to bring the spacecraft from an higher radius orbit to Umbriel's circular orbit with minimum $\Delta$V budget.
\section{Introduction}
The mission is carried out in two phases:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{The Interplanetary Phase}: We depart from Earth's LEO to reach a HUO (High Uranus Orbit), using two gravity assists from Mars and Jupiter.
    \item  \textbf{The Low Thrust Trajectory Phase}:We depart from a circular orbit with radius\\ $r_i=466000\,km$ and we arrive at another circular orbit with radius $r_f=R_{Umbriel}$.
\end{itemize}
\section{Interplanetary Phase Model}
\subsection{Approximations}
We carry out our analysis using some approximations:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We operate in a two dimensional environment.
    \item We use the patched conics approach. This means that we consider only the Central Body inside of its Sphere of Influence. The definition of SoI is the static one \\$r_{SoI,B}=(\frac{\mu_B}{\mu_{Sun}})^{\frac{2}{5}}\cdot d(B,Sun)$.
    \item We consider the planets with a constant density. We don't consider Perturbations due to Gravity Gradients.
    \item We ignore the Ephemeris problem. We consider that the Planets that we reach are present once we arrive at their respective radius.
    \item We consider a mean distance from the Sun. Most planets have slightly non circular orbits, so we approximate all planet orbits exactly at $e=0$.
    \item The equatorial plane of Uranus is inclined of 98° w.r.t Ecliptic Plane. As we do all the assists in the Ecliptic Plane, we will consider that after the last gravity assist we impose a perturbation that will make us arrive at the desired radius at the equator of Uranus.
\end{enumerate}
