\subsection{Shooting Method}
To find the costate variables that we need to minimize the fuel consumption, we use a function of the calculator that finds the minimum of a function given  some boundaries. The boundaries can be Upper or Lower ones ,for the parameters that we are searching for, but mostly non linear constraints that the parameters have to comply with. This equations will contain the boundaries that we know of. The optimizer will need a first guess of the parameters that we have to find, in which there will be the final time and $C_f$, which is the prime parameter that the optimizer must minimize.
\subsubsection{Non Linear Constraints}
The Optimizier will try to reach zero for all the non linear constraints.
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \begin{cases}
        r_f=R_{target}\\
        v_{r_f}=0\\
        v_{\theta_f}=v_{\theta_{target}}\\
        H_f=0\\
        p_{C_f}=-1
    \end{cases}
    \quad \rightarrow NLC=
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \alpha\,(r_f-R_{target})\\
       \beta\, (v_{r_f})\\
       \gamma\, (v_{\theta_f}-v_{\theta_{target}})\\
        H_f\\
        p_{C_f}+1
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{eqnarray*}
We added some terms to normalize the constraints. In our case $\alpha=10^{-3}$, $\beta=100$, $\gamma=10$.
\subsubsection{Initial Guesses}
The Optimizer is really sensitive to initial guess changes, so before starting to input random parameters there are some methods to refine the guess.
\begin{itemize}
    \item As the radial velocity will be always very small, we take as an initial guess of $p_{v_{r_0}}$a value near zero and as we want to diminish the radius, we take a negative value.
    \item The tangential velocity must be higher, but to have a smoother curve we don't take a really high value of $p_{v_{\theta_0}}$. We choose a negative sign for the same reason of the radial velocity costate.
    \item We take $p_{C_0}$ near -1.
    \item The final time will be much higher than a Homann transfer.
    \item As a good approximation we take $C_f=\frac{1}{2}\Gamma_{max}\cdot t_f$
\end{itemize}
