\section{Conclusions}
In this work, a preliminary mission design towards Umbriel was developed, with the primary objective of testing the efficacy of different orbital optimization methods. The analysis was structured into two distinct but complementary phases: an interplanetary phase and a low-thrust breaking phase.\\
For the interplanetary leg, the use of the patched conics approach and the exploitation of gravity assist maneuvers on Mars and Jupiter allowed reaching Uranus' orbit while keeping the propellant budget within acceptable limits, with a total time of flight of approximately 20 years.\\
Subsequently, the application of Pontryagin's Maximum Principle allowed for the optimization of the low-thrust trajectory for insertion into the final orbit. \\
Numerical results confirm the validity of the approach: the optimized maneuver requires a duration of about 34 days and a $\Delta V$ of only $1.03$ km/s, which is lower than the $1.12$ km/s required by an equivalent Hohmann transfer.Overall, the proposed mission strategy led to a drastic reduction in the total energy cost.\\
Compared to an estimate of $16.27$ km/s required for a standard bi-elliptic transfer without gravity assists and a Homann breaking phase, our optimized solution reduced the cost to $8.791$ km/s. This net saving of $7.48$ km/s demonstrates how the integration of assisted maneuvers and continuous thrust optimization are fundamental for the feasibility of missions towards the outer solar system.
